# newt-python metadata leak gate — hashed denylist for repository METADATA only.
#
# One sha256 hex digest per line, of a lowercased token (or of two adjacent tokens
# joined). scripts/leak_gate_metadata.py tokenizes every remote branch name and every
# open pull-request title and compares digests, against this file UNION the content list
# in leak_terms.sha256 — a branch named after internal infrastructure is as public as one
# named after an internal lane.
#
# Why a second file instead of more lines in leak_terms.sha256: these terms must NOT fire
# on file contents. They name internal work lanes, and tracked comments on main cite those
# lanes' card IDs as provenance for why a behaviour is the way it is — legitimate, useful,
# and roughly three dozen lines across a dozen files. Folding these digests into the
# content list would fail the content gate on main immediately. Metadata is a different
# surface, so it takes a different denylist.
#
# Why hashed, and why committed here rather than fetched at runtime: same reasoning as the
# content list — a public file listing our internal terms would be a map of what to grep
# our history for, and this is obscurity, not secrecy. Fetching the plaintext from the
# private repo instead would need a secret, and secrets are withheld from workflow runs on
# pull requests from forks: the check would skip exactly where an outside contributor
# might introduce something, and a gate that silently skips is worse than no gate.
#
# Plaintext source + regeneration command live in the private portal repo at
# wiki/operating-docs/leak-gate-metadata-terms.txt. Edit there, regenerate, commit both.

